From f6d9a767dccc9a82d7baec7d4b5353ffdb7549e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 16:06:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(sliders): normalise frame sizes with sharp, not Pillow
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The installer resized the one mismatched pair with Pillow. The Codespace has no
Python imaging library, so it died with ModuleNotFoundError, `screen-recoat`
kept its 1712x1152 before against a 1168x784 after, and verify-sliders.mjs
failed the run — correctly, and for exactly the reason it was written.

sharp 0.34.5 is already in node_modules; Next depends on it. So the resize needs
no install at all, and the step that had no business failing no longer can.

scripts/normalise-slider-frames.mjs walks both frame directories, compares each
pair with sharp's own metadata, and resizes only where the two disagree. It is
idempotent — 58 pairs checked, nothing touched when they already match — so it
is safe after every unzip. The guard's message now names the command instead of
telling a human to go and find an image editor.

Verified through the whole cycle rather than from the passing end: the original
1712x1152 frames were restored from the archive, the guard failed with the exact
production message, the normaliser fixed both files, and the guard passed. An
earlier attempt at this test copied from a directory that no longer existed and
"passed" without ever reintroducing the defect.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
